---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const breakevenCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'p*x', label: 'revenue = price × units', color: '#c2410c', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: 'f + v*x', label: 'total cost = fixed + variable × units', color: '#374151' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'p', label: 'price p', min: 5, max: 100, step: 1, value: 40 },
    { name: 'v', label: 'variable cost per unit v', min: 1, max: 80, step: 1, value: 25 },
    { name: 'f', label: 'fixed costs f', min: 0, max: 6000, step: 50, value: 2400 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 500, label: 'units sold' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/foundational/unit-economics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every business, from a tea stall to a trillion-dollar platform, lives or dies on one
    calculation: does a <strong>unit</strong> of what you sell (one cup, one subscription, one
    ride) bring in more money than it costs to deliver? Revenue rises with price and volume;
    costs split into <strong>fixed</strong> (rent, salaries — paid even at zero sales) and
    <strong>variable</strong> (materials, per-unit delivery). Where the two curves cross is the
    <strong>break-even point</strong> — the sales you must make before the business earns
    anything at all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the circled crossing is survival — drag price, costs and see exactly how many units it takes" widget="plotter" config={breakevenCfg} />

  <p>
    Try the experiments: raise the price p and the break-even point slides left (fewer sales
    needed — but will customers stay?). Cut variable cost v and the gap between the lines widens
    — every unit after break-even earns the gap as profit. Double the fixed costs f and the whole
    cost line rises: this is why "high-fixed-cost" businesses (airlines, chip fabs) live or die
    on volume.
  </p>

  <h2>The margin you sell and the margin you keep</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contribution margin</strong> = price − variable cost. Each unit "contributes" this much toward fixed costs; only after they're covered does it become profit.</li>
    <li><strong>Gross vs net.</strong> Gross profit subtracts delivery costs; net profit subtracts everything, including the fixed costs many founders forget they pay themselves.</li>
    <li><strong>Cash ≠ profit.</strong> A profitable business can die of bad timing — salaries are due monthly, customers may pay in 90 days. Working capital is survival.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth at a loss</strong> is only rational if unit economics become positive at scale — the discipline behind "blitzscaling", and the cause of most startup post-mortems when it wasn't true.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard managerial accounting treatments; Horngren's Cost Accounting; Osterwalder, Business Model Generation.</p>
</TopicPage>
